CORE
main.cpp
--cpp20
^\[main\.assertion\.1\] line \d+ disjunction-constrained conversion operator: SUCCESS$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
--
^CONVERSION ERROR$
^.*is unknown.*$
--
[temp.constr.op]/[temp.deduct]/5: a conversion-function template whose
requires-clause is a disjunction (integral<T> or floating_point<T>) is removed
from the candidate set when the whole constraint is unsatisfied with the deduced
arguments -- for a class destination both disjuncts are false.

The constraint check now evaluates the boolean structure, so the non-viable
candidate for the mutually-recursive class destination is correctly rejected
(no spurious operator-to-class instantiation).  Minimal model of libstdc++'s
__max_size_type / __max_diff_type conversion operators.
